Resource: Sunil | Name: CL-CRP-001229 | Match Text: In patients with CKD, JARDIANCE slowed the eGFR decline across the spectrum of uACR | Country of Origin: Corporate | Brand: JARDIANCE | Therapeutic Area: Metabolism | Lifecycle State: Approved for Use | Indication: Chronic kidney disease | Key Message Type: Efficacy | Footnotes: In the EMPA-KIDNEY trial, a randomised, parallel-group, double-blind, placebo-controlled study of 6609 patients with CKD, the efficacy and safety of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=3304) were evaluated vs placebo (n=3305). Patients were adults with an eGFR ≥ 20, &lt; 45 mL/min/1.73 m2 ; or an eGFR ≥ 45, &lt; 90 mL/min/1.73 m2 with a uACR ≥ 200 mg/g. The primary endpoint in the EMPA-KIDNEY trial was a composite of CV death or progression of kidney disease defined as end-stage kidney disease (the initiation of maintenance dialysis or receipt of a kidney transplant), a sustained decrease in the eGFR to &lt;10 ml/min/1.73 m2, a sustained decrease in eGFR of ≥40% from baseline, or death from renal causes. Patients treated with JARDIANCE experienced a 28% RRR in this endpoint (HR=0.72; 95% CI: 0.64, 0.82; p&lt;0.001).  Annual rate of change in eGFR was a prespecified exploratory outcome in the EMPA-KIDNEY trial. Shown are the results of long-term slope, representing the mean (±SE) changes from 2 months after the first dose of JARDIANCE or placebo to the final follow-up visit.   CI=confidence interval; CKD=chronic kidney disease; CV=cardiovascular; eGFR=estimated glomerular filtration; HR=hazard ratio; RRR=relative risk reduction; SE=standard error; uACR=urinary albumin-to-creatinine ratio. | Reference: Herrington 2022 NEJM, Nov 4, 2022 | AMA Style Guide References: Herrington 2022 N Engl J Med, Nov 4, 2022. | Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets: NA
