<node id="urn:kg:jardiance:section:092b58c9-a6f2-4452-abf5-8d057c058ab5">
  <data key="d0">brand</data>
  <data key="d20">MEDICATION GUIDE JARDIANCE ®  (jar DEE ans) (empagliflozin tablets)</data>
  <data key="d19">heading</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
  <data key="d9">medication_guide</data>
  <data key="d10">092b58c9-a6f2-4452-abf5-8d057c058ab5</data>
  <data key="d11">docling</data>
  <data key="d12">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
  <data key="d14">dd225975-2d03-4997-adf6-8e9d59ecfa0e</data>
  <data key="d16">for oral use</data>
  <data key="d3">Section</data>
  <data key="d4">jardiance</data>
  <data key="d5">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
</node>
<node id="urn:kg:jardiance:section:9243128c-062c-41e1-9bc8-5e082dc68fe6">
  <data key="d0">brand</data>
  <data key="d20">What is the most important information I should know about JARDIANCE?</data>
  <data key="d19">heading</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
  <data key="d9">medication_guide</data>
  <data key="d10">9243128c-062c-41e1-9bc8-5e082dc68fe6</data>
  <data key="d11">docling</data>
  <data key="d12">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
  <data key="d14">dd225975-2d03-4997-adf6-8e9d59ecfa0e</data>
  <data key="d16">JARDIANCE can cause serious side effects, including:

- Diabetic ketoacidosis (increased ketones in your blood or urine) in people with type 1 and other

ketoacidosis. JARDIANCE can cause ketoacidosis that can be life-threatening and may lead to death. Ketoacidosis is a serious condition which needs to be treated in a hospital. People with type 1 diabetes have a high risk of getting ketoacidosis. People with type 2 diabetes or pancreas problems also have an increased risk of getting ketoacidosis. Ketoacidosis can also happen in people who: are sick, cannot eat or drink as usual, skip meals, are on a diet high in fat and low in carbohydrates (ketogenic diet), take less than the usual amount of insulin or miss insulin doses, drink too much alcohol, have a loss of too much fluid from the body (volume depletion), or who have surgery. Ketoacidosis can happen even if your blood sugar is less than 250 mg/dL. Your healthcare provider may ask you to periodically check ketones in your urine or blood.

Stop taking JARDIANCE and call your healthcare provider or get medical help right away if you get any of the following. If possible, check for ketones in your urine or blood, even if your blood sugar is less than 250 mg/dL:

- o nausea
- o vomiting
- o stomach-area (abdominal) pain
- o tiredness
- o trouble breathing
- o ketones in your urine or blood
- Dehydration. JARDIANCE can cause some people to become dehydrated (the loss of body water and salt). Dehydration may cause you to feel dizzy, faint, light-headed, or weak, especially when you stand up (orthostatic hypotension). There have been reports of sudden worsening of kidney function in people who are taking JARDIANCE. You may be at higher risk of dehydration if you:
- o take medicines to lower your blood pressure, including diuretics (water pills)
- o are on low sodium (salt) diet
- o have kidney problems
- o are 65 years of age or older

Talk to your healthcare provider about what you can do to prevent dehydration including how much fluid you should drink on a daily basis. Call your healthcare provider right away if you reduce the amount of food or liquid you drink, for example if you are sick or you cannot eat, or start to lose liquids from your body, for example from vomiting, diarrhea or being in the sun too long.</data>
  <data key="d3">Section</data>
  <data key="d4">jardiance</data>
  <data key="d5">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
</node>
<node id="urn:kg:jardiance:section:3d49f702-5f1a-4179-8011-ef47ce1d86fd">
  <data key="d0">brand</data>
  <data key="d20">What is JARDIANCE?</data>
  <data key="d19">heading</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
  <data key="d9">medication_guide</data>
  <data key="d10">3d49f702-5f1a-4179-8011-ef47ce1d86fd</data>
  <data key="d11">docling</data>
  <data key="d12">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
  <data key="d14">dd225975-2d03-4997-adf6-8e9d59ecfa0e</data>
  <data key="d16">JARDIANCE is a prescription medicine used to:

- o reduce the risk of cardiovascular death and hospitalization for heart failure in adults with heart failure, when the heart cannot pump enough blood to the rest of your body.
- o reduce the risk of further worsening of kidney disease, end-stage kidney disease (ESKD), death due to cardiovascular disease, and hospitalization in adults with chronic kidney disease.
- o reduce the risk of cardiovascular death in adults with type 2 diabetes and who also have known cardiovascular disease.
- o lower blood sugar (glucose) along with diet and exercise in adults and children who are 10 years of age and older with type 2 diabetes.

JARDIANCE is not for use to lower blood sugar (glucose) in people with type 1 diabetes. It may increase their risk of diabetic ketoacidosis (increased ketones in blood or urine).

JARDIANCE is not for use to lower blood sugar (glucose) in people with type 2 diabetes who have severe kidney problems, because it may not work.

JARDIANCE is not for people with polycystic kidney disease, or who are taking or have recently received certain types of immunosuppressive therapy to treat kidney disease. JARDIANCE is not expected to work if you have these conditions.

It is not known if JARDIANCE is safe and effective in children under 10 years of age.</data>
  <data key="d3">Section</data>
  <data key="d4">jardiance</data>
  <data key="d5">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
</node>
<node id="urn:kg:jardiance:section:196880d4-b9cd-44af-abbd-43021dea64ce">
  <data key="d0">brand</data>
  <data key="d20">Who should not take JARDIANCE? Do not take JARDIANCE if you:</data>
  <data key="d19">heading</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
  <data key="d9">medication_guide</data>
  <data key="d10">196880d4-b9cd-44af-abbd-43021dea64ce</data>
  <data key="d11">docling</data>
  <data key="d12">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
  <data key="d14">dd225975-2d03-4997-adf6-8e9d59ecfa0e</data>
  <data key="d16">- are allergic to empagliflozin or any of the ingredients in JARDIANCE.  See the end of this Medication Guide for a complete list of ingredients in JARDIANCE. Symptoms of a serious allergic reaction to JARDIANCE may include:
- o rash
- o raised, red areas on your skin (hives)
- o swelling of your face, lips, mouth, and throat that may cause difficulty in breathing or swallowing

If you have any of these symptoms, stop taking JARDIANCE and call your healthcare provider right away or go to the nearest hospital emergency room.</data>
  <data key="d3">Section</data>
  <data key="d4">jardiance</data>
  <data key="d5">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
</node>
<node id="urn:kg:jardiance:section:1e466430-38ec-4714-ad1f-2ee4b88c0c6d">
  <data key="d0">brand</data>
  <data key="d20">What should I tell my healthcare provider before taking JARDIANCE?</data>
  <data key="d19">heading</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
  <data key="d9">medication_guide</data>
  <data key="d10">1e466430-38ec-4714-ad1f-2ee4b88c0c6d</data>
  <data key="d11">docling</data>
  <data key="d12">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
  <data key="d14">dd225975-2d03-4997-adf6-8e9d59ecfa0e</data>
  <data key="d16"></data>
  <data key="d3">Section</data>
  <data key="d4">jardiance</data>
  <data key="d5">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
</node>
<node id="urn:kg:jardiance:section:22b6a12e-7336-4962-a213-b1b681d4a256">
  <data key="d0">brand</data>
  <data key="d20">Before taking JARDIANCE, tell your healthcare provider about all of your medical conditions, including if</data>
  <data key="d19">heading</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
  <data key="d9">medication_guide</data>
  <data key="d10">22b6a12e-7336-4962-a213-b1b681d4a256</data>
  <data key="d11">docling</data>
  <data key="d12">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
  <data key="d14">dd225975-2d03-4997-adf6-8e9d59ecfa0e</data>
  <data key="d16"></data>
  <data key="d3">Section</data>
  <data key="d4">jardiance</data>
  <data key="d5">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
</node>
<node id="urn:kg:jardiance:section:0060633e-350c-47c6-926e-2d8d46f60767">
  <data key="d0">brand</data>
  <data key="d20">you:</data>
  <data key="d19">heading</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
  <data key="d9">medication_guide</data>
  <data key="d10">0060633e-350c-47c6-926e-2d8d46f60767</data>
  <data key="d11">docling</data>
  <data key="d12">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
  <data key="d14">dd225975-2d03-4997-adf6-8e9d59ecfa0e</data>
  <data key="d16">- have type 1 diabetes or have had diabetic ketoacidosis.
- have a decrease in your insulin dose.
- have a serious infection.
- have a history of infection of the vagina or penis.
- have a history of amputation.
- have kidney problems.
- have liver problems.
- have a history of urinary tract infections or problems with urination.
- are on a low sodium (salt) diet. Your healthcare provider may change your diet or your dose.
- are going to have surgery. Your healthcare provider may stop your JARDIANCE before you have surgery. Talk to your healthcare provider if you are having surgery about when to stop taking JARDIANCE and when to start it again.
- are eating less, or there is a change in your diet.
- are dehydrated.
- have or have had problems with your pancreas, including pancreatitis or surgery on your pancreas.
- drink alcohol very often or drink a lot of alcohol in the short term ('binge' drinking).
- have ever had an allergic reaction to JARDIANCE.
- are pregnant or plan to become pregnant. JARDIANCE may harm your unborn baby. If you become pregnant while taking JARDIANCE, tell your healthcare provider as soon as possible. Talk with your healthcare provider about the best way to control your blood sugar while you are pregnant.
- are breastfeeding or plan to breastfeed. JARDIANCE may pass into your breast milk and may harm your baby. Talk with your healthcare provider about the best way to feed your baby if you are taking JARDIANCE.  Do not breastfeed while taking JARDIANCE.

Tell your healthcare provider about all the medicines you take, including prescription and over-the-counter medicines, vitamins, and herbal supplements.

JARDIANCE may affect the way other medicines work, and other medicines may affect how JARDIANCE works. Know the medicines you take. Keep a list of them to show your healthcare provider and pharmacist when you get a new medicine.</data>
  <data key="d3">Section</data>
  <data key="d4">jardiance</data>
  <data key="d5">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
</node>
<node id="urn:kg:jardiance:section:d964cc3f-8dec-4a55-9a7f-744d85b37fc9">
  <data key="d0">brand</data>
  <data key="d20">How should I take JARDIANCE?</data>
  <data key="d19">heading</data>
  <data key="d8">2</data>
  <data key="d9">medication_guide</data>
  <data key="d10">d964cc3f-8dec-4a55-9a7f-744d85b37fc9</data>
  <data key="d11">docling</data>
  <data key="d12">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
  <data key="d14">dd225975-2d03-4997-adf6-8e9d59ecfa0e</data>
  <data key="d16">- Take JARDIANCE exactly as your healthcare provider tells you to take it.
- Take JARDIANCE by mouth 1 time in the morning each day, with or without food.
- Your healthcare provider will tell you how much JARDIANCE to take and when to take it.  Your healthcare provider may change your dose if needed.
- Your healthcare provider may tell you to take JARDIANCE along with other diabetes medicines. Low blood sugar can happen more often when JARDIANCE is taken with certain other diabetes medicines. See 'What are the possible side effects of JARDIANCE?'
- If you miss a dose, take it as soon as you remember.  If it is almost time for your next dose, skip the missed dose and take the medicine at the next regularly scheduled time.  Do not take two doses of JARDIANCE at the same time.  Talk with your healthcare provider if you have questions about a missed dose.
- If you take too much JARDIANCE, call your healthcare provider or Poison Help line at 1-800-222-1222, or go to the nearest hospital emergency room right away.
- When taking JARDIANCE, you may have sugar in your urine, which will show up on a urine test.
- When your body is under some types of stress, such as fever, trauma (such as a car accident), infection, or surgery, the amount of diabetes medicine you need may change. Tell your healthcare provider right away if you have any of these conditions and follo w your healthcare provider's instructions.
- Your healthcare provider may do certain blood tests before you start JARDIANCE and during treatment as needed.</data>
  <data key="d3">Section</data>
  <data key="d4">jardiance</data>
  <data key="d5">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
</node>
<node id="urn:kg:jardiance:section:21357259-c329-4301-84ad-57a3387e1f09">
  <data key="d0">brand</data>
  <data key="d20">What are the possible side effects of JARDIANCE?</data>
  <data key="d19">heading</data>
  <data key="d8">3</data>
  <data key="d9">medication_guide</data>
  <data key="d10">21357259-c329-4301-84ad-57a3387e1f09</data>
  <data key="d11">docling</data>
  <data key="d12">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
  <data key="d14">dd225975-2d03-4997-adf6-8e9d59ecfa0e</data>
  <data key="d16">JARDIANCE may cause serious side effects, including:

- See ' What is the most important information I should know about JARDIANCE? '
- Genital and urinary tract infections. JARDIANCE can cause serious infections in your genital area or urinary tract that could require hospitalization. A rare but serious bacterial infection called necrotizing fasciitis can cause damage to the tissue under the skin in the area between and around the anus and genitals (perineum). This infection may require hospitalization, multiple surgeries, and could lead to death. Seek medical attention immediately if you have a fever or you are feeling very weak, tired or uncomfortable (malaise), and you develop any of the following symptoms in the area between and around your anus and genitals:
- o pain or tenderness
- o swelling
- o redness of skin (erythema)

Also tell your healthcare provider if you have any of these signs or symptoms of urinary tract infections or yeast infections:

- o Urinary tract infection:
- burning feeling when you urinate
- need to urinate often or right away
- pain in the lower part of your stomach (pelvis)
- blood in your urine

You may also have a fever, back pain, nausea, or vomiting.</data>
  <data key="d3">Section</data>
  <data key="d4">jardiance</data>
  <data key="d5">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
</node>
<node id="urn:kg:jardiance:section:a98514df-eea8-4c25-b0bc-4ee951a0679c">
  <data key="d0">brand</data>
  <data key="d20">o Vaginal yeast infection :</data>
  <data key="d19">heading</data>
  <data key="d8">3</data>
  <data key="d9">medication_guide</data>
  <data key="d10">a98514df-eea8-4c25-b0bc-4ee951a0679c</data>
  <data key="d11">docling</data>
  <data key="d12">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
  <data key="d14">dd225975-2d03-4997-adf6-8e9d59ecfa0e</data>
  <data key="d16">- vaginal odor
- white or yellowish vaginal discharge (may be lumpy or look like cottage cheese)
- vaginal itching
- o Yeast infection of the skin around the penis (balanitis or balanoposthitis): If you are uncircumcised, swelling may make it difficult to pull back the skin around the tip of your penis. Other symptoms include:
- redness, itching, or swelling of the penis
- bad smelling discharge from the penis
- rash on the penis
- pain in the skin around the penis

Talk to your healthcare provider about what to do if you get symptoms of a yeast infection. They may suggest you use an over-the-counter antifungal medicine. Contact your healthcare provider right away if your symptoms do not improve after using an over-the-counter antifungal medicine.

- Low blood sugar (hypoglycemia). In adults, if you take JARDIANCE with another medicine that can cause low blood sugar, such as a sulfonylurea or insulin, your risk of getting low blood sugar is higher. In children 10 years of age and older, the risk for low blood sugar is higher with JARDIANCE even if you do not use another medicine that can also lower blood sugar. The dose of your sulfonylurea medicine or insulin may need to be lowered while you take JARDIANCE.

Signs and symptoms of low blood sugar may include:

- o headache

- [ ] o irritability

- [ ] o confusion

- o dizziness

- o drowsiness

- [ ] o hunger

- o shaking or feeling jittery

- o sweating

- o weakness

- [ ] o fast heartbeat

- Amputations. SGLT2 inhibitors may increase your risk of lower limb amputations.

You may be at a higher risk of lower limb amputation if you:

- o have a history of amputation
- o have had blocked or narrowed blood vessels, usually in your leg
- o have had diabetic foot infection, ulcers or sores

Call your healthcare provider right away if you have new pain or tenderness, any sores, ulcers, or infections in your leg or foot. Talk to your healthcare provider about proper foot care.

- Serious allergic reactions. If you have any symptoms of a serious allergic reaction, stop taking JARDIANCE and call your healthcare provider right away or go to the nearest hospital emergency room. See ' Who should not take JARDIANCE? ' .</data>
  <data key="d3">Section</data>
  <data key="d4">jardiance</data>
  <data key="d5">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
</node>
<node id="urn:kg:jardiance:section:196c6afe-997c-4f73-86fd-53ad9901c9a9">
  <data key="d0">brand</data>
  <data key="d20">The most common side effects of JARDIANCE include:</data>
  <data key="d19">heading</data>
  <data key="d8">3</data>
  <data key="d9">medication_guide</data>
  <data key="d10">196c6afe-997c-4f73-86fd-53ad9901c9a9</data>
  <data key="d11">docling</data>
  <data key="d12">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
  <data key="d14">dd225975-2d03-4997-adf6-8e9d59ecfa0e</data>
  <data key="d16">- urinary tract infections
- yeast infections in females

These are not all the possible side effects of JARDIANCE.  For more information, ask your healthcare provider or pharmacist.

Call your doctor for medical advice about side effects.  You may report side effects to FDA at 1-800-FDA-1088.</data>
  <data key="d3">Section</data>
  <data key="d4">jardiance</data>
  <data key="d5">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
</node>
<node id="urn:kg:jardiance:section:8278adc3-cb3a-4e67-bb7b-a6352be74bd8">
  <data key="d0">brand</data>
  <data key="d20">How should I store JARDIANCE?</data>
  <data key="d19">heading</data>
  <data key="d8">3</data>
  <data key="d9">medication_guide</data>
  <data key="d10">8278adc3-cb3a-4e67-bb7b-a6352be74bd8</data>
  <data key="d11">docling</data>
  <data key="d12">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
  <data key="d14">dd225975-2d03-4997-adf6-8e9d59ecfa0e</data>
  <data key="d16">- Store JARDIANCE at room temperature between 68 ° F to 77 ° F (20 ° C to 25 ° C).
- Keep JARDIANCE and all medicines out of the reach of children.</data>
  <data key="d3">Section</data>
  <data key="d4">jardiance</data>
  <data key="d5">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
</node>
<node id="urn:kg:jardiance:section:29d81d7e-8e15-48bc-aa7a-1050d980ebe7">
  <data key="d0">brand</data>
  <data key="d20">General information about the safe and effective use of JARDIANCE.</data>
  <data key="d19">heading</data>
  <data key="d8">4</data>
  <data key="d9">medication_guide</data>
  <data key="d10">29d81d7e-8e15-48bc-aa7a-1050d980ebe7</data>
  <data key="d11">docling</data>
  <data key="d12">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
  <data key="d14">dd225975-2d03-4997-adf6-8e9d59ecfa0e</data>
  <data key="d16">Medicines are sometimes prescribed for purposes other than those listed in a Medication Guide. Do not use JARDIANCE for a condition for which it is not prescribed.  Do not give JARDIANCE to other people, even if they have the same symptoms you have.  It may harm them.

You can ask your pharmacist or healthcare provider for information about JARDIANCE that is written for health professionals.</data>
  <data key="d3">Section</data>
  <data key="d4">jardiance</data>
  <data key="d5">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
</node>
<node id="urn:kg:jardiance:section:076bfd5a-0799-41c4-98b1-e61017972290">
  <data key="d0">brand</data>
  <data key="d20">What are the ingredients in JARDIANCE?</data>
  <data key="d19">heading</data>
  <data key="d8">4</data>
  <data key="d9">medication_guide</data>
  <data key="d10">076bfd5a-0799-41c4-98b1-e61017972290</data>
  <data key="d11">docling</data>
  <data key="d12">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
  <data key="d14">dd225975-2d03-4997-adf6-8e9d59ecfa0e</data>
  <data key="d16">Active Ingredient:

empagliflozin

Inactive Ingredients: colloidal silicon dioxide, croscarmellose sodium, hydroxypropyl cellulose, lactose monohydrate, magnesium stearate, and microcrystalline cellulose.  In addition, the film coating contains the following inactive ingredients: ferric oxide yellow, hypromellose, polyethylene glycol, talc, and titanium dioxide.

Distributed by: Boehringer Ingelheim Pharmaceuticals, Inc., Ridgefield, CT 06877 USA

Licensed from: Boehringer Ingelheim International GmbH, Ingelheim, Germany

JARDIANCE is a registered trademark of and used under license from Boehringer Ingelheim International GmbH.

Boehringer Ingelheim Pharmaceuticals, Inc. either owns or uses the EMPA-REG OUTCOME ® , EMPEROR-Reduced ® , EMPEROR-Preserved ® , and EMPA-KIDNEY ® trademarks under license.

The other brands listed are trademarks of their respective owners and are not trademarks of Boehringer Ingelheim Pharmaceuticals, Inc.

Copyright © 2026 Boehringer Ingelheim International GmbH ALL RIGHTS RESERVED

COL8914QA062026

For more information about JARDIANCE, including current prescribing information and Medication Guide, go to www.jardiance.com , scan the code, or call Boehringer Ingelheim Pharmaceuticals, Inc. at 1-800-542-6257.

&lt;!-- image --&gt;

This Medication Guide has been approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration.

Revised: January 2026</data>
  <data key="d3">Section</data>
  <data key="d4">jardiance</data>
  <data key="d5">Medication Guidance.pdf</data>
</node>
<node id="urn:kg:jardiance:table:bdbcac76-9e19-4e55-8ae3-ad3e318ca123">
  <data key="d0">brand</data>
  <data key="d43">0</data>
  <data key="d8">1</data>
  <data key="d9">claims_content_modules</data>
  <data key="d11">openpyxl</data>
  <data key="d12">Jardiance_Claims_updated &amp; Content Modules 1.xlsx</data>
  <data key="d42">Sheet</data>
  <data key="d28">bdbcac76-9e19-4e55-8ae3-ad3e318ca123</data>
  <data key="d14">e77ccb64-a3a2-41c2-9027-fb5e54716ebc</data>
  <data key="d44">{"sheet_name": "Sheet", "columns": ["Resource", "Name", "Match Text", "Country of Origin", "Brand", "Therapeutic Area", "Lifecycle State", "Indication", "Key Message Type", "End Audience", "Contextual Information", "Footnotes", "Reference", "AMA Style Guide References", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets"], "rows": [{"row_number": 2, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-000958", "Match Text": "In patients with CKD, JARDIANCE reduced the relative risk of CV death or kidney disease progression by 28%", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "In the EMPA-KIDNEY trial, a randomised, parallel-group, double-blind, placebo-controlled study of 6609 patients with CKD, the efficacy and safety of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=3304) were evaluated vs placebo (n=3305). Patients were adults with an eGFR ≥ 20, &lt; 45 mL/min/1.73 m2 ; or an eGFR ≥ 45, &lt; 90 mL/min/1.73 m2 with a uACR ≥ 200 mg/g. The primary endpoint in the EMPA-KIDNEY trial was a composite of CV death or progression of kidney disease defined as end-stage kidney disease (the initiation of maintenance dialysis or receipt of a kidney transplant), a sustained decrease in the eGFR to &lt;10 ml/min/1.73 m2, a sustained decrease in eGFR of ≥40% from baseline, or death from renal causes. Patients treated with JARDIANCE experienced a 28% RRR in this endpoint (HR=0.72; 95% CI: 0.64, 0.82; p&lt;0.001).  CI=confidence interval; CKD=chronic kidney disease; eGFR=estimated glomerular filtration;  HR=hazard ratio; RRR=relative risk reduction; T2D=type 2 diabetes; uACR=urinary albumin-to-creatinine ratio.", "Reference": "Herrington 2022 NEJM, Nov 4, 2022", "AMA Style Guide References": "Herrington 2022 N Engl J Med, Nov 4, 2022.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "CPNT-CRP-100323 JARDIANCE (v3.0)"}}, {"row_number": 3, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-000960", "Match Text": "In patients with HF with LVEF &gt;40%, JARDIANCE reduced the relative risk of CV death or HHF by 21%", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "In the EMPEROR-Preserved trial, a randomised, double-blind, parallel-group, placebo-controlled study of 5988 patients with HFpEF, the efficacy and safety profile of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=2997) was evaluated vs placebo (n=2991). Patients were adults with chronic heart failure (NYHA class II, III, or IV) and preserved ejection fraction (LVEF &gt; 40%). The primary endpoint in the EMPEROR-Preserved trial was a composite of CV death or HHF, analysed as time to the first event. Patients treated with JARDIANCE experienced a 21% RRR in this endpoint (HR=0.79; 95% CI: 0.69, 0.90; p&lt;0.001).  CI=confidence interval; CV=cardiovascular;  HFpEF=heart failure with preserved ejection fraction; HHF=hospitalization for heart failure; HR=hazard ratio; LVEF=left ventricular ejection fraction;  NYHA=New York Heart Association; RRR=relative risk reduction.", "Reference": "RD-157961 Anker SD, Butler J, Filippatos G, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(16):1451-1461", "AMA Style Guide References": "Anker SD, Butler J, Filippatos G, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(16):1451-1461.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "CPNT-CRP-100331 JARDIANCE (v1.0)"}}, {"row_number": 4, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-000964", "Match Text": "In patients with CKD, JARDIANCE reduced the relative risk of first and recurrent all-cause hospitalisation by 14%", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "In the EMPA-KIDNEY trial, a randomised, parallel-group, double-blind, placebo-controlled study of 6609 patients with CKD, the efficacy and safety of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=3304) were evaluated vs placebo (n=3305). Patients were adults with an eGFR ≥ 20, &lt; 45 mL/min/1.73 m2 ; or an eGFR ≥ 45, &lt; 90 mL/min/1.73 m2 with a uACR ≥ 200 mg/g. The primary endpoint in the EMPA-KIDNEY trial was a composite of CV death or progression of kidney disease defined as end-stage kidney disease (the initiation of maintenance dialysis or receipt of a kidney transplant), a sustained decrease in the eGFR to &lt;10 ml/min/1.73 m2, a sustained decrease in eGFR of ≥40% from baseline, or death from renal causes. Patients treated with JARDIANCE experienced a 28% RRR in this endpoint (HR=0.72; 95% CI: 0.64, 0.82; p&lt;0.001).  Hospitalisation for any cause was a key secondary outcome of the EMPA-KIDNEY trial. The analysis of hospitalisations for any cause included the first and all subsequent events (JARDIANCE, 1611 hospitalisations in 960 patients; placebo, 1895 hospitalisations in 1035 patients).  CI=confidence interval; CKD=chronic kidney disease; eGFR=estimated glomerular filtration;  HR=hazard ratio; RRR=relative risk reduction; uACR=urinary albumin-to-creatinine ratio.", "Reference": "Herrington 2022 NEJM, Nov 4, 2022", "AMA Style Guide References": "Herrington 2022 N Engl J Med, Nov 4, 2022.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "\nCPNT-CRP-100326 JARDIANCE (v1.0)"}}, {"row_number": 5, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-000966", "Match Text": "In patients with LVEF ≤ 40%, JARDIANCE slowed the decline of renal function by 4 times", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "In the EMPEROR-Reduced trial, a randomised, double-blind, parallel-group, placebo-controlled study of 3730 patients with HFrEF, the efficacy and safety profile of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=1863) was evaluated vs placebo (n=1867). Patients were adults with chronic heart failure (NYHA class II, III, or IV) and reduced ejection fraction (LVEF ≤ 40%). The primary endpoint in the EMPEROR-Reduced trial was a composite of CV death or HHF, analysed as time to the first event. Patients treated with JARDIANCE experienced a 25% RRR in this endpoint (HR=0.75; 95% CI: 0.65, 0.86; p&lt;0.001).   The rate of decline in eGFR was a prespecified secondary outcome of the EMPEROR-Reduced trial.  CI=confidence interval; CV=cardiovascular; eGFR=estimated glomerular filtration rate; HFrEF=heart failure with reduced ejection fraction; HHF=hospitalization for heart failure; HR=hazard ratio; LVEF=left ventricular ejection fraction;  NYHA=New York Heart Association; RRR=relative risk reduction", "Reference": "RD-157832 JARDIANCE M. Packer, S.D. Anker, J. Butler; Cardiovascular and Renal Outcomes with Empagli", "AMA Style Guide References": "Packer M, Anker SD, Butler J. Cardiovascular and Renal Outcomes with Empagli.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 6, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-000968", "Match Text": "In patients with LVEF &gt; 40%, JARDIANCE slowed the decline of renal function by 2 times", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "In the EMPEROR-Preserved trial, a randomised, double-blind, parallel-group, placebo-controlled study of 5988 patients with HFpEF, the efficacy and safety profile of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=2997) was evaluated vs placebo (n=2991). Patients were adults with chronic heart failure (NYHA class II, III, or IV) and preserved ejection fraction (LVEF &gt; 40%). The primary endpoint in the EMPEROR-Preserved trial was a composite of CV death or HHF, analysed as time to the first event. Patients treated with JARDIANCE experienced a 21% RRR in this endpoint (HR=0.79; 95% CI: 0.69, 0.90; p&lt;0.001).  The rate of decline in eGFR was a prespecified secondary outcome of the EMPEROR-Preserved trial.  CI=confidence interval; CV=cardiovascular; eGFR=estimated glomerular filtration rate; HFpEF=heart failure with preserved ejection fraction; HHF=hospitalization for heart failure; HR=hazard ratio; LVEF=left ventricular ejection fraction;  NYHA=New York Heart Association; RRR=relative risk reduction", "Reference": "RD-157961 Anker SD, Butler J, Filippatos G, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(16):1451-1461", "AMA Style Guide References": "Anker SD, Butler J, Filippatos G, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(16):1451-1461.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 7, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-000969", "Match Text": "In patients with T2D and CVD, JARDIANCE reduced the relative risk of CV death by 38%", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "The primary composite outcome in the EMPA-REG OUTCOME trial was 3-point MACE, composed of death from CV causes, nonfatal MI, or nonfatal stroke, as analyzed in the pooled JARDIANCE group vs the placebo group. Patients were adults with insufficiently controlled T2D and CAD, PAD, or a history of MI or stroke. The 14% RRR in 3-point MACE (HR=0.86; 95% CI: 0.74, 0.99; p&lt;0.001 for noninferiority; p=0.04 for superiority) was driven by a reduction in the risk of CV death (HR=0.62; 95% CI: 0.49, 0.77) and 38% RRR in CV death was achieved in the overall EMPA-REG OUTCOME population for the duration of the trial.  Pooled data from 10-mg and 25-mg doses of JARDIANCE; both doses showed a comparable reduction in the risk of CV death.  In addition to reducing the risk of CV death when added to the standard of care, JARDIANCE also lowered HbA1c. In addition, JARDIANCE demonstrated reduction in weight and blood pressure. JARDIANCE is not indicated for weight loss or reduction of blood pressure.  CAD=coronary artery disease; CI=confidence interval; CV=cardiovascular; eCVD=established cardiovascular disease; HbA1c=haemoglobin A1c; HR=hazard ratio; MACE=major adverse cardiovascular events; MI=myocardial infarction; PAD=peripheral artery disease; RRR=relative risk reduction; SGLT2i=sodium-glucose cotransporter-2 inhibitor; T2D=type 2 diabetes.", "Reference": "\nRD-103048 Zinman B, Wanner C, Lachin JM, et al. New Eng J Med. 2016;373:2117-28.", "AMA Style Guide References": "Zinman B, Wanner C, Lachin JM, et al. N Engl J Med. 2016;373:2117-28.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "\nCPNT-CRP-100327 JARDIANCE (v2.0)"}}, {"row_number": 8, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-000959", "Match Text": "In patients with HF with LVEF ≤40%, JARDIANCE reduced the relative risk of CV death or HHF by 25%", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "In the EMPEROR-Reduced trial, a randomised, double-blind, parallel-group, placebo-controlled study of 3730 patients with HFrEF, the efficacy and safety profile of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=1863) was evaluated vs placebo (n=1867). Patients were adults with chronic heart failure (NYHA class II, III, or IV) and reduced ejection fraction (LVEF ≤ 40%). The primary endpoint in the EMPEROR-Reduced trial was a composite of CV death or HHF, analysed as time to the first event. Patients treated with JARDIANCE experienced a 25% RRR in this endpoint (HR=0.75; 95% CI: 0.65, 0.86; p&lt;0.001).  CI=confidence interval; CV=cardiovascular;  HFrEF=heart failure with reduced ejection fraction; HHF=hospitalization for heart failure; HR=hazard ratio; LVEF=left ventricular ejection fraction;  NYHA=New York Heart Association; RRR=relative risk reduction", "Reference": "RD-157832 JARDIANCE M. Packer, S.D. Anker, J. Butler; Cardiovascular and Renal Outcomes with Empagli", "AMA Style Guide References": "Packer M, Anker SD, Butler J. Cardiovascular and Renal Outcomes with Empagli.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "CPNT-CRP-100329 JARDIANCE (v1.0)"}}, {"row_number": 9, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-000965", "Match Text": "In patients with LVEF ≤ 40%, JARDIANCE reduced the relative risk of first and recurrent HHF by 30%", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "In the EMPEROR-Reduced trial, a randomised, double-blind, parallel-group, placebo-controlled study of 3730 patients with HFrEF, the efficacy and safety profile of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=1863) was evaluated vs placebo (n=1867). Patients were adults with chronic heart failure (NYHA class II, III, or IV) and reduced ejection fraction (LVEF ≤ 40%). The primary endpoint in the EMPEROR-Reduced trial was a composite of CV death or HHF, analysed as time to the first event. Patients treated with JARDIANCE experienced a 25% RRR in this endpoint (HR=0.75; 95% CI: 0.65, 0.86; p&lt;0.001).   The occurrence of all HHF, including first and recurrent events, was a prespecified secondary outcome of the EMPEROR-Reduced trial.  In addition to the RRR of all HHF, including first and recurrent events, the risk of total hospitalisations for any reason was also significantly reduced (HR=0.85, 95% CI: 0.75, 0.95).  CI=confidence interval; CV=cardiovascular;  HFrEF=heart failure with reduced ejection fraction; HHF=hospitalization for heart failure; HR=hazard ratio; LVEF=left ventricular ejection fraction;  NYHA=New York Heart Association; RRR=relative risk reduction", "Reference": "RD-157832 JARDIANCE M. Packer, S.D. Anker, J. Butler; Cardiovascular and Renal Outcomes with Empagli", "AMA Style Guide References": "Packer M, Anker SD, Butler J. Cardiovascular and Renal Outcomes with Empagli.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "CPNT-CRP-100330 JARDIANCE (v2.0)"}}, {"row_number": 10, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-000967", "Match Text": "In patients with LVEF &gt; 40%, JARDIANCE reduced the relative risk of first and recurrent HHF by 27%", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "In the EMPEROR-Preserved trial, a randomised, double-blind, parallel-group, placebo-controlled study of 5988 patients with HFpEF, the efficacy and safety profile of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=2997) was evaluated vs placebo (n=2991). Patients were adults with chronic heart failure (NYHA class II, III, or IV) and preserved ejection fraction (LVEF &gt; 40%). The primary endpoint in the EMPEROR-Preserved trial was a composite of CV death or HHF, analysed as time to the first event. Patients treated with JARDIANCE experienced a 21% RRR in this endpoint (HR=0.79; 95% CI: 0.69, 0.90; p&lt;0.001).  The occurrence of all HHF, including first and recurrent events, was a prespecified secondary outcome of the EMPEROR-Preserved trial.  CI=confidence interval; CV=cardiovascular;  HFpEF=heart failure with preserved ejection fraction; HHF=hospitalization for heart failure; HR=hazard ratio; LVEF=left ventricular ejection fraction;  NYHA=New York Heart Association; RRR=relative risk reduction", "Reference": "RD-157961 Anker SD, Butler J, Filippatos G, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(16):1451-1461", "AMA Style Guide References": "Anker SD, Butler J, Filippatos G, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(16):1451-1461.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "CPNT-CRP-100332 JARDIANCE (v1.0)"}}, {"row_number": 11, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-000971", "Match Text": "The interconnected nature of cardio, renal and metabolic diseases ampliﬁes and accelerates risk.", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": null, "Reference": "2020 - Cardiorenal Syndrome in Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus - Rational Use f Sodium-glucose Cotransporte", "AMA Style Guide References": "Cardiorenal Syndrome in Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus - Rational Use f Sodium-glucose Cotransporte. 2020.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 12, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-000973", "Match Text": "JARDIANCE has a proven safety and tolerability profile across multiple trials.", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Safety", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "Please see the SmPC for the full list of AEs and full list of special warnings and precautions for use, as well as dosing details.  AE=adverse event; SmPC=Summary of Product Characteristics", "Reference": "Jardiance - EU/SmPC ", "AMA Style Guide References": "- EU/SmPC.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 13, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-000978", "Match Text": "Adopting guideline-recommended treatments improves patient outcomes.", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Guidelines", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": null, "Reference": "2023 - Contemporary Use of Sodium-Glucose Cotransporter-2 Inhibitor Therapy Among Patients Hospitali ", "AMA Style Guide References": "Contemporary Use of Sodium-Glucose Cotransporter-2 Inhibitor Therapy Among Patients Hospitali. 2023.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 14, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-000979", "Match Text": "Despite their proven potential, evidence-based recommendations are often underutilized", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Guidelines", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": null, "Reference": "2023 - Contemporary Use of Sodium-Glucose Cotransporter-2 Inhibitor Therapy Among Patients Hospitali \n\n2019 - Reasons for Lack of Improvement in Treatment With Evidence-Based Therapies in Heart Failure", "AMA Style Guide References": "Contemporary Use of Sodium-Glucose Cotransporter-2 Inhibitor Therapy Among Patients Hospitali. 2023.\n\nReasons for Lack of Improvement in Treatment With Evidence-Based Therapies in Heart Failure. 2019.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 15, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001161", "Match Text": "In patients with HF, JARDIANCE delivered consistent efficacy regardless of T2D or CKD status", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "In the EMPEROR-Reduced trial, a randomised, double-blind, parallel-group, placebo-controlled study of 3730 patients with HFrEF, the efficacy and safety profile of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=1863) was evaluated vs placebo (n=1867). Patients were adults with chronic heart failure (NYHA class II, III, or IV) and reduced ejection fraction (LVEF ≤ 40%). The primary endpoint in the EMPEROR-Reduced trial was a composite of CV death or HHF, analysed as time to the first event. Patients treated with JARDIANCE experienced a 25% RRR in this endpoint (HR=0.75; 95% CI: 0.65, 0.86; p&lt;0.001).   Results of prespecified subgroup analyses in the EMPEROR-Reduced trial. Diabetes at baseline (HR=0.72; 95% CI: 0.60, 0.87); no diabetes at baseline (HR=0.78; 95% CI: 0.64, 0.97). eGFR (CKD-EPI) ≥ 60 mL/min/1.73 m2 at baseline (HR=0.67; 95% CI: 0.55, 0.83); eGFR (CKD-EPI) &lt; 60 mL/min/1.73 m2 at baseline (HR=0.83; 95% CI: 0.69, 1.00).  In the EMPEROR-Preserved trial, a randomised, double-blind, parallel-group, placebo-controlled study of 5988 patients with HFpEF, the efficacy and safety profile of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=2997) was evaluated vs placebo (n=2991). Patients were adults with chronic heart failure (NYHA class II, III, or IV) and preserved ejection fraction (LVEF &gt; 40%). The primary endpoint in the EMPEROR-Preserved trial was a composite of CV death or HHF, analysed as time to the first event. Patients treated with JARDIANCE experienced a 21% RRR in this endpoint (HR=0.79; 95% CI: 0.69, 0.90; p&lt;0.001).  Results of prespecified subgroup analyses in the EMPEROR-Preserved trial. Diabetes at baseline (HR=0.79; 95% CI: 0.67, 0.94); no diabetes at baseline (HR=0.78; 95% CI: 0.64, 0.95). eGFR (CKD-EPI) ≥ 60 mL/min/1.73 m2 at baseline (HR=0.81; 95% CI: 0.65, 1.00); eGFR (CKD-EPI) &lt; 60 mL/min/1.73 m2 at baseline (HR=0.78; 95% CI: 0.66, 0.91).  CI=confidence interval;  CV=cardiovascular; eGFR=estimated glomerular filtration;  HFpEF=heart failure with preserved ejection fraction; HFrEF=heart failure with reduced ejection fraction; HHF=hospitalisation for heart failure;  HR=hazard ratio; LVEF=left ventricular ejection fraction; NYHA=New York Heart Association; RRR=relative risk reduction; T2D=type 2 diabetes..", "Reference": "RD-142505 JARDIANCE Packer 2020_Cardiovascular and renal outcomes \n\nRD-157961 Anker SD, Butler J, Filippatos G, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(16):1451-1461", "AMA Style Guide References": "Packer 2020 Cardiovascular and renal outcomes.\n\nAnker SD, Butler J, Filippatos G, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(16):1451-1461.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 16, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001157", "Match Text": "JARDIANCE protected patients with HF by reducing the risk of CV death or HHF", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "In the EMPEROR-Reduced trial, a randomised, double-blind, parallel-group, placebo-controlled study of 3730 patients with HFrEF, the efficacy and safety profile of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=1863) was evaluated vs placebo (n=1867). Patients were adults with chronic heart failure (NYHA class II, III, or IV) and reduced ejection fraction (LVEF ≤ 40%). The primary endpoint in the EMPEROR-Reduced trial was a composite of CV death or HHF, analysed as time to the first event. Patients treated with JARDIANCE experienced a 25% RRR in this endpoint (HR=0.75; 95% CI: 0.65, 0.86; p&lt;0.001).  In the EMPEROR-Preserved trial, a randomised, double-blind, parallel-group, placebo-controlled study of 5988 patients with HFpEF, the efficacy and safety profile of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=2997) was evaluated vs placebo (n=2991). Patients were adults with chronic heart failure (NYHA class II, III, or IV) and preserved ejection fraction (LVEF &gt; 40%). The primary endpoint in the EMPEROR-Preserved trial was a composite of CV death or HHF, analysed as time to the first event. Patients treated with JARDIANCE experienced a 21% RRR in this endpoint (HR=0.79; 95% CI: 0.69, 0.90; p&lt;0.001).  CI=confidence interval;  CV=cardiovascular; HF=heart failure; HHFpEF=heart failure with preserved ejection fraction; HFrEF=heart failure with reduced ejection fraction; HHF=hospitalisation for heart failure;  HR=hazard ratio; LVEF=left ventricular ejection fraction; NYHA=New York Heart Association; RRR=relative risk reduction.", "Reference": "RD-142505 JARDIANCE Packer 2020_Cardiovascular and renal outcomes\n\nRD-157961 Anker SD, Butler J, Filippatos G, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(16):1451-1461", "AMA Style Guide References": "Packer 2020 Cardiovascular and renal outcomes.\n\nAnker SD, Butler J, Filippatos G, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(16):1451-1461.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 17, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001158", "Match Text": "In patients with HF, JARDIANCE protected the kidneys by slowing the decline of renal function", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "In the EMPEROR-Reduced trial, a randomised, double-blind, parallel-group, placebo-controlled study of 3730 patients with HFrEF, the efficacy and safety profile of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=1863) was evaluated vs placebo (n=1867). Patients were adults with chronic heart failure (NYHA class II, III, or IV) and reduced ejection fraction (LVEF ≤ 40%). The primary endpoint in the EMPEROR-Reduced trial was a composite of CV death or HHF, analysed as time to the first event. Patients treated with JARDIANCE experienced a 25% RRR in this endpoint (HR=0.75; 95% CI: 0.65, 0.86; p&lt;0.001).  In the EMPEROR-Preserved trial, a randomised, double-blind, parallel-group, placebo-controlled study of 5988 patients with HFpEF, the efficacy and safety profile of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=2997) was evaluated vs placebo (n=2991). Patients were adults with chronic heart failure (NYHA class II, III, or IV) and preserved ejection fraction (LVEF &gt; 40%). The primary endpoint in the EMPEROR-Preserved trial was a composite of CV death or HHF, analysed as time to the first event. Patients treated with JARDIANCE experienced a 21% RRR in this endpoint (HR=0.79; 95% CI: 0.69, 0.90; p&lt;0.001).  The rate of decline in eGFR was a prespecified secondary outcome of the EMPEROR-Preserved and EMPEROR-Reduced trials.  CI=confidence interval;  CV=cardiovascular; eGFR=estimated glomerular filtration; HF=heart failure; HFpEF=heart failure with preserved ejection fraction; HFrEF=heart failure with reduced ejection fraction; HHF=hospitalisation for heart failure;  HR=hazard ratio; LVEF=left ventricular ejection fraction; NYHA=New York Heart Association; RRR=relative risk reduction.", "Reference": "RD-142505 JARDIANCE Packer 2020_Cardiovascular and renal outcomes\n\nRD-157961 Anker SD, Butler J, Filippatos G, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(16):1451-1461", "AMA Style Guide References": "Packer 2020 Cardiovascular and renal outcomes.\n\nAnker SD, Butler J, Filippatos G, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(16):1451-1461.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 18, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001160", "Match Text": "JARDIANCE demonstrated early benefits when initiated in the hospital", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Safety", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "In the EMPULSE trial, a randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled study of 530 patients with chronic heart failure regardless of LVEF, the efficacy and safety of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=265) were evaluated in the hospital setting vs placebo (n=265). The primary endpoint in the EMPULSE trial was clinical benefit, defined as a hierarchical composite of death from any cause, number of heart failure events and time to first heart failure event, or a 5-point or greater difference in change from baseline in the KCCQ-TSS at 90 days, as assessed using a win ratio. Patients treated with JARDIANCE were 36% more likely to experience clinical benefit compared with placebo (win ratio=1.36; 95% CI: 1.09, 1.68; p=0.0054).  In the EMPULSE trial, the initiation of empagliflozin for acute HF produced clinical benefit regardless of the degree of symptomatic impairment at baseline and improved symptoms, physical limitations, and quality of life, with benefits seen as early as 15 days and maintained through 90 days.  CI=confidence interval; KCCQ-TSS=Kansas City Cardiomyopathy Questionnaire Total Symptom Score; LVEF=left ventricular ejection fraction; NYHA=New York Heart Association.", "Reference": "\nRD-157613 JARDIANCE Voors Nature Medicines\n\n\n25. Kosiborod MN. Circulation. 2022;146(4):279-288.\n", "AMA Style Guide References": "Voors Nat Med.\n\nKosiborod MN. Circulation. 2022;146(4):279-288.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 19, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001172", "Match Text": "In patients with T2D and eCVD, JARDIANCE reduced the relative risk of incident or worsening nephropathy by 39%", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "In the EMPA-REG OUTCOME trial, a randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled study of 7720 patients with T2D at high cardiovascular risk, the efficacy and safety of JARDIANCE 10 mg or 25 mg (n=4867) were evaluated vs placebo (n=2333). Patients were adults with insufficiently controlled T2D and CAD, PAD, or a history of MI or stroke. The primary composite outcome in the EMPA-REG OUTCOME trial was 3-point MACE, composed of death from CV causes, nonfatal MI, or nonfatal stroke, as analysed in the pooled JARDIANCE group vs the placebo group. The result was 14% RRR (1.6% ARR) in 3-point MACE (HR=0.86; 95% CI: 0.74, 0.99; p&lt;0.001 for noninferiority; p=0.04 for superiority).   Incident or worsening nephropathy is defined as progression to macroalbuminuria, doubling of serum creatinine, eGFR of ≤ 45 mL/min/1.73 m2; initiation of renal replacement therapy; death from renal disease. Incident or worsening nephropathy was a prespecified component of the secondary microvascular outcome in the EMPA-REG OUTCOME trial.  CAD=coronary artery disease; CI=confidence interval; CV=cardiovascular;  eGFR=estimated glomerular filtration; HR=hazard ratio; MACE=major adverse cardiovascular events; MI=myocardial infarction; PAD=peripheral artery disease; RRR=relative risk reduction; T2D=type 2 diabetes.", "Reference": "2015 - Empagliflozin, Cardiovascular Outcomes, and Mortality in Type 2 Diabetes\n\nRD-149832 JARDIANCE Christoph Wanner The New England Journal of Medicine", "AMA Style Guide References": "Empagliflozin, Cardiovascular Outcomes, and Mortality in Type 2 Diabetes. 2015.\n\nChristoph Wanner The New England Journal of Medicine.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "\nCPNT-CRP-100328 JARDIANCE (v1.0)"}}, {"row_number": 20, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001165", "Match Text": "Prescribe JARDIANCE to reduce kidney disease progression or risk of CV death in patients with CKD", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "In the EMPA-KIDNEY trial, a randomised, parallel-group, double-blind, placebo-controlled study of 6609 patients with CKD, the efficacy and safety of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=3304) were evaluated vs placebo (n=3305). Patients were adults with an eGFR ≥ 20, &lt; 45 mL/min/1.73 m2 ; or an eGFR ≥ 45, &lt; 90 mL/min/1.73 m2 with a uACR ≥ 200 mg/g. The primary endpoint in the EMPA-KIDNEY trial was a composite of CV death or progression of kidney disease defined as end-stage kidney disease (the initiation of maintenance dialysis or receipt of a kidney transplant), a sustained decrease in the eGFR to &lt;10 ml/min/1.73 m2, a sustained decrease in eGFR of ≥40% from baseline, or death from renal causes. Patients treated with JARDIANCE experienced a 28% RRR in this endpoint (HR=0.72; 95% CI: 0.64, 0.82; p&lt;0.001).2  CI=confidence interval; CKD=chronic kidney disease; CV=cardiovascular; eGFR=estimated glomerular filtration rate; HR=hazard ratio; RRR=relative risk reduction uACR=urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio.", "Reference": "Herrington 2022 NEJM, Nov 4, 2022 \n\nJardiance - SmPC", "AMA Style Guide References": "Herrington 2022 N Engl J Med, Nov 4, 2022.\n\n- SmPC.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 21, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001166", "Match Text": "EMPA-KIDNEY was the largest and broadest SGLT2i trial of its kind", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": "HCP (Health Care Personnel)", "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "CKD=chronic kidney disease; SGLT2i=sodium glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor", "Reference": "Herrington 2022 NEJM, Nov 4, 2022 \n\nEMPA-KIDNEY Collaborative Group Nephrol Dial Transplant 2022", "AMA Style Guide References": "Herrington 2022 N Engl J Med, Nov 4, 2022.\n\nEMPA-KIDNEY Collaborative Group Nephrol Dial Transplant. 2022.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 22, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001171", "Match Text": "In patients with T2D and CVD, JARDIANCE reduced the relative risk of HHF by 35%", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "The primary composite outcome in the EMPA-REG OUTCOME trial was 3-point MACE, composed of death from CV causes, nonfatal MI, or nonfatal stroke, as analyzed in the pooled JARDIANCE group vs the placebo group. Patients were adults with insufficiently controlled T2D and CAD, PAD, or a history of MI or stroke. The 14% RRR in 3-point MACE (HR=0.86; 95% CI: 0.74, 0.99; p&lt;0.001 for noninferiority; p=0.04 for superiority) was driven by a reduction in the risk of CV death (HR=0.62; 95% CI: 0.49, 0.77).  Hospitalisation for heart failure was a secondary CV outcome in the EMPA-REG OUTCOME trial (HR=0.65; 95% CI: 0.50, 0.85).  CAD=coronary artery disease; CI=confidence interval; CV=cardiovascular; HHF=hospitalization for heart failure; HR=hazard ratio; MACE=major adverse cardiovascular events; MI=myocardial infarction; PAD=peripheral artery disease; RRR=relative risk reduction; T2D=type 2 diabetes.", "Reference": "2015 - Empagliflozin, Cardiovascular Outcomes, and Mortality in Type 2 Diabetes", "AMA Style Guide References": "Empagliflozin, Cardiovascular Outcomes, and Mortality in Type 2 Diabetes. 2015.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 23, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001184", "Match Text": "JARDIANCE offers triple protection for cardiovascular, renal, and metabolic conditions", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": "HCP (Health Care Personnel)", "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": null, "Reference": "Herrington 2022 NEJM, Nov 4, 2022\n\n2015 - Empagliflozin, Cardiovascular Outcomes, and Mortality in Type 2 Diabetes\n\nRD-142505 JARDIANCE Packer 2020_Cardiovascular and renal outcomes\n\nRD-157961 Anker SD, Butler J, Filippatos G, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(16):1451-1461", "AMA Style Guide References": "Herrington 2022 N Engl J Med, Nov 4, 2022.\n\nEmpagliflozin, Cardiovascular Outcomes, and Mortality in Type 2 Diabetes. 2015.\n\nPacker 2020 Cardiovascular and renal outcomes.\n\nAnker SD, Butler J, Filippatos G, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(16):1451-1461.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 24, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001188", "Match Text": "JARDIANCE may provide the greatest kidney benefit and delay of dialysis when initiated early", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": "HCP (Health Care Personnel)", "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "According to a calculation based on the hypothetical transformation of chronic eGFR slopes into time to kidney failure in an independent publication.  Estimated from each baseline eGFR value by applying the chronic eGFR slopes corresponding to participants on placebo vs JARDIANCE within the prespecified eGFR subgroups (eGFR cutoff points to define subgroups).  CKD=chronic kidney disease; eGFR=estimated glomerular filtration rate.", "Reference": "2023 - EMPA-KIDNEY: expanding the range of kidney protection by SGLT2 inhibitors", "AMA Style Guide References": "EMPA-KIDNEY: expanding the range of kidney protection by SGLT2 inhibitors. 2023.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 25, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001192", "Match Text": "CKD is a silent, progressive disease often missed and untreated in early phases", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "CKD=chronic kidney disease.", "Reference": "\n2024 KDIGO Guidelines ", "AMA Style Guide References": "2024 KDIGO Guidelines.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 26, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001196", "Match Text": "JARDIANCE is an easy choice to help protect your patients by reducing CV and renal risk", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Dosing and Administration", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "CV=cardiovascular", "Reference": "\nJardiance - EU/SmPC", "AMA Style Guide References": "- EU/SmPC.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 27, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001197", "Match Text": "JARDIANCE is easy to use across a broad spectrum of patients", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": null, "Reference": "\nJardiance - EU/SmPC", "AMA Style Guide References": "- EU/SmPC.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 28, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001195", "Match Text": "Proactively assess kidney health using uACR and eGFR in patients with hypertension or T2D", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "CRM/Cardio-Renal-Metabolic, Disease Awareness", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "eGFR=estimated glomerular filtration rate; T2D=type 2 diabetes; uACR=urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio", "Reference": "2024 KDIGO Guidelines \n\nGaitonde Am Fam Physician 2017", "AMA Style Guide References": "2024 KDIGO Guidelines.\n\nGaitonde Am Fam Physician. 2017.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 29, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001193", "Match Text": "Risk of CV death increases with declining kidney function", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "CRM/Cardio-Renal-Metabolic, Disease Awareness", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "CV=cardiovascular", "Reference": "2012 - Chronic Kidney Disease Prognosis Consortium. Association of kidney disease measures with mort ", "AMA Style Guide References": "Chronic Kidney Disease Prognosis Consortium. Association of kidney disease measures with mort. 2012.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 30, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001198", "Match Text": "JARDIANCE dosing recommendation based on indication and kidney function", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Dosing and Administration", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "CV=cardiovascular", "Reference": "\nJardiance - EU/SmPC", "AMA Style Guide References": "- EU/SmPC.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 31, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001201", "Match Text": "More than 100 nephrology, endocrinology, and cardiology guidelines, worldwide, endorse the foundational use of SGLT2is, such as JARDIANCE", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Guidelines", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "SGLT2i=sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor.", "Reference": "2023 - Local/regional guidelines where Empagliflozin/SGLT-2i are recommended", "AMA Style Guide References": "Local/regional guidelines where Empagliflozin/SGLT-2i are recommended. 2023.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 32, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001200", "Match Text": "JARDIANCE is 1A recommended for first-line treatment of CKD, with or without T2D", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "CRM/Cardio-Renal-Metabolic, Disease Awareness", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "CKD=chronic kidney disease; T2D=type 2 diabetes", "Reference": "2024 KDIGO Guidelines", "AMA Style Guide References": "2024 KDIGO Guidelines.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 33, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001202", "Match Text": "International CKD, T2D, and HF guidelines recommend the use of an SGLT2i, such as JARDIANCE, as early as first line", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Guidelines", "End Audience": "HCP (Health Care Personnel)", "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "CKD=chronic kidney disease; CV=cardiovascular HF=heart failure; SGLT2i=sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor. T2D=type 2 diabetes", "Reference": "2022 - ADAEASD ConsensusReport_Davies MJ et al, Diabetes Care 2022\n\n2022 - de Boer IH et al., Diabetes Care 2022; 45(12): 3075–90\n\n2024 KDIGO Guidelines\n\nRD-151098 JARDIANCE McDonagh, Metra 2021 ESC HF Guidelines\n\nMcDonagh TA, Metra M, Adamo M, et al. Eur Heart J 2023 \n\nFreinkel Standard of care in Diabetes 2025\n\nSeidu S i wsp. Prim Care Diabetes 2022;16:223\n\nRD-169473 McDonagh TA et al. 2021 Eur Heart J. 2021;42(36):3599-3726\n\nMarx Eur Heart J 2023", "AMA Style Guide References": "ADAEASD ConsensusReport Davies MJ et al, Diabetes Care 2022. 2022.\n\nde Boer IH et al., Diabetes Care 2022. 45(12): 3075–90. 2022.\n\n2024 KDIGO Guidelines.\n\nMcDonagh, Metra 2021 ESC HF Guidelines.\n\nMcDonagh TA, Metra M, Adamo M, et al. Eur Heart J. 2023.\n\nFreinkel Standard of care in Diabetes 2025.\n\nSeidu S i wsp. Prim Care Diabetes. 2022;16:223.\n\nMcDonagh TA et al. 2021 Eur Heart J. 2021;42(36):3599-3726.\n\nMarx Eur Heart J. 2023.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 34, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001203", "Match Text": "Heart failure, regardless of rEF or pEF, is a progressive condition that needs urgent, optimal intervention", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Guidelines", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "pEF=preserved ejection fraction; rEF=reduced ejection fraction", "Reference": "11. Shah KS et al. J Am Coll Cardiol. 2017;70(20):2476-2486\n\nRD-171272 Schwinger_Cardiovascular Diagnosis and Therapy_2021\n\nAbdin ESC Heart Fail 2021\n\nMaddox JACC 2024", "AMA Style Guide References": "Shah KS et al. J Am Coll Cardiol. 2017;70(20):2476-2486.\n\nSchwinger Cardiovascular Diagnosis and Therapy. 2021.\n\nAbdin ESC Heart Fail. 2021.\n\nMaddox J Am Coll Cardiol. 2024.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 35, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001204", "Match Text": "1 in 10 patients will die within 1 year after diagnosis of HF", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": null, "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "HF=heart failure", "Reference": "2015 - Mortality in heart failure patients", "AMA Style Guide References": "Mortality in heart failure patients. 2015.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 36, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001207", "Match Text": "Reduced eGFR is associated with increased risk of all-cause mortality, CV death, and HHF in patients with HF", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": null, "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "CV=cardiovascular; eGFR=estimated glomerular filatration rate; HF=heart failure; HHF=hospitalization for heart failure", "Reference": "\n2019 - Heart failure in chronic kidney disease: conclusions from a Kidney Disease", "AMA Style Guide References": "Heart failure in chronic kidney disease: conclusions from a Kidney Disease. 2019.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 37, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001211", "Match Text": "In adult patients with HF across the LVEF spectrum, JARDIANCE demonstrated early benefits when initiated in hospital within 1-5 days after stabilization", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Safety", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "Adult patients with chronic heart failure (NYHA class II, III, or IV) and reduced ejection fraction (LVEF ≤40%).  Adult patients with chronic heart failure (NYHA class II, III, or IV) and preserved ejection fraction (LVEF &gt;40%).  In the EMPULSE trial, a randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled study of 530 patients with chronic heart failure regardless of LVEF, the efficacy and safety of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=265) were evaluated in the hospital setting vs placebo (n=265). The primary endpoint in the EMPULSE trial was clinical benefit, defined as a hierarchical composite of death from any cause, number of heart failure events and time to first heart failure event, or a 5-point or greater difference in change from baseline in the KCCQ-TSS at 90 days, as assessed using a win ratio. Patients treated with JARDIANCE were 36% more likely to experience clinical benefit compared with placebo (win ratio=1.36; 95% CI: 1.09, 1.68; p=0.0054).   CI=confidence interval; KCCQ-TSS=Kansas City Cardiomyopathy Questionnaire Total Symptom Score; LVEF=left ventricular ejection fraction; NYHA=New York Heart Association.", "Reference": "RD-157832 JARDIANCE M. Packer, S.D. Anker, J. Butler; Cardiovascular and Renal Outcomes with Empagli \n\nRD-157961 Anker SD, Butler J, Filippatos G, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(16):1451-1461\n\nRD-157613 JARDIANCE Voors Nature Medicines", "AMA Style Guide References": "Packer M, Anker SD, Butler J. Cardiovascular and Renal Outcomes with Empagli.\n\nAnker SD, Butler J, Filippatos G, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(16):1451-1461.\n\nVoors Nat Med.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 38, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001205", "Match Text": "Approximately 1 in 4 patients hospitalized for HF are readmitted within 30 days", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": null, "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "HF=heart failure; LVEF=left ventricular ejection fraction", "Reference": "2020 - Reducing All-cause 30-day Hospital Readmissions for Patients Pres", "AMA Style Guide References": "Reducing All-cause 30-day Hospital Readmissions for Patients Pres. 2020.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 39, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001206", "Match Text": "Patients with HF had lower HRQoL compared to those at risk of HF", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": null, "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "HF=heart failure;HRQoL=health-related quality of life", "Reference": "2020 - Change of Health-Related Quality of Life Over Time and Its Association With Patient Outcomes", "AMA Style Guide References": "Change of Health-Related Quality of Life Over Time and Its Association With Patient Outcomes. 2020.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 40, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001210", "Match Text": "JARDIANCE delivered consistent efficacy regardless of T2D or CKD status of patients with LVEF &gt;40%", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "In the EMPEROR-Preserved trial, a randomised, double-blind, parallel-group, placebo-controlled study of 5988 patients with HFpEF, the efficacy and safety profile of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=2997) was evaluated vs placebo (n=2991). Patients were adults with chronic heart failure (NYHA class II, III, or IV) and preserved ejection fraction (LVEF &gt; 40%). The primary endpoint in the EMPEROR-Preserved trial was a composite of CV death or HHF, analysed as time to the first event. Patients treated with JARDIANCE experienced a 21% RRR in this endpoint (HR=0.79; 95% CI: 0.69, 0.90; p&lt;0.001).  Results of prespecified subgroup analyses in the EMPEROR-Preserved trial. Diabetes at baseline (HR=0.79; 95% CI: 0.67, 0.94); no diabetes at baseline (HR=0.78; 95% CI: 0.64, 0.95). eGFR (CKD-EPI) ≥ 60 mL/min/1.73 m2 at baseline (HR=0.81; 95% CI: 0.65, 1.00); eGFR (CKD-EPI) &lt; 60 mL/min/1.73 m2 at baseline (HR=0.78; 95% CI: 0.66, 0.91).  CI=confidence interval;  CKD=chronic kidney disease; CKD-EPI=Chronic Kidney Disease Epidemiology Collaboration; CV=cardiovascular; eGFR=estimated glomerular filtration; HFpEF=heart failure with preserved ejection fraction; HHF=hospitalisation for heart failure;  HR=hazard ratio; LVEF=left ventricular ejection fraction; NYHA=New York Heart Association; RRR=relative risk reduction; T2D=type 2 diabetes.", "Reference": "RD-157961 Anker SD, Butler J, Filippatos G, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(16):1451-1461", "AMA Style Guide References": "Anker SD, Butler J, Filippatos G, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(16):1451-1461.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 41, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001208", "Match Text": "JARDIANCE demonstrated significant protection as early as 2 weeks by reducing the risk of CV death or HHF across the LVEF spectrum", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "In the EMPEROR-Reduced trial, a randomised, double-blind, parallel-group, placebo-controlled study of 3730 patients with HFrEF, the efficacy and safety profile of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=1863) was evaluated vs placebo (n=1867). Patients were adults with chronic heart failure (NYHA class II, III, or IV) and reduced ejection fraction (LVEF ≤ 40%). The primary endpoint in the EMPEROR-Reduced trial was a composite of CV death or HHF, analysed as time to the first event. Patients treated with JARDIANCE experienced a 25% RRR in this endpoint (HR=0.75; 95% CI: 0.65, 0.86; p&lt;0.001).  In the EMPEROR-Reduced trial, the effect of empagliflozin to reduce the combined risk of death, hospitalisation for heart failure, or an emergent or urgent heart failure visit was statistically significant at 12 days after randomisation and statistical significance was sustained from day 12 onward (HR, 0.70; 95% CI, 0.63-0.78; p&lt;0.0001).  In the EMPEROR-Preserved trial, a randomised, double-blind, parallel-group, placebo-controlled study of 5988 patients with HFpEF, the efficacy and safety profile of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=2997) was evaluated vs placebo (n=2991). Patients were adults with chronic heart failure (NYHA class II, III, or IV) and preserved ejection fraction (LVEF &gt; 40%). The primary endpoint in the EMPEROR-Preserved trial was a composite of CV death or HHF, analysed as time to the first event. Patients treated with JARDIANCE experienced a 21% RRR in this endpoint (HR=0.79; 95% CI: 0.69, 0.90; p&lt;0.001).   In the EMPEROR-Preserved trial, the effect of empagliflozin on time to cardiovascular death or heart failure hospitalization was statistically significant at 18 days after randomisation and statistical significance was sustained from day 18 onward (HR=0.41, 95% CI 0.17–0.99; p=0.0476).  CI=confidence interval; CV=cardiovascular; HF=heart failure; HFpEF=heart failure with preserved ejection fraction; HFrEF=heart failure with reduced ejection fraction; HHF=hospitalisation for heart failure;  HR=hazard ratio; LVEF=left ventricular ejection fraction; NYHA=New York Heart Association; RRR=relative risk reduction", "Reference": "RD-157832 JARDIANCE M. Packer, S.D. Anker, J. Butler; Cardiovascular and Renal Outcomes with Empagli \n\n2021 - Effect of Empagliflozin on the Clinical Stability of Patients With Heart Failure and a Reduce\n\nRD-157961 Anker SD, Butler J, Filippatos G, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(16):1451-1461\n\n2022 - Early benefit with empagliflozin in heart failure with preserved ejection fraction: insights", "AMA Style Guide References": "Packer M, Anker SD, Butler J. Cardiovascular and Renal Outcomes with Empagli.\n\nEffect of Empagliflozin on the Clinical Stability of Patients With Heart Failure and a Reduce. 2021.\n\nAnker SD, Butler J, Filippatos G, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385(16):1451-1461.\n\nEarly benefit with empagliflozin in heart failure with preserved ejection fraction: insights. 2022.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 42, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001212", "Match Text": "JARDIANCE is an easy choice to protect your patients with HF by reducing CV and renal risk", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "CV=cardiovascular; HF=heart failure", "Reference": "Jardiance - EU/SmPC", "AMA Style Guide References": "- EU/SmPC.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 43, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001214", "Match Text": "SGLT2is, such as JARDIANCE, are Class 1 recommended for first-line use in patients with eCVD and T2D like Joana to reduce the risk of CV death and hospitalizations, independent of glucose control", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Guidelines", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "CV=cardiovascular; eCVD=established cardiovascular disease; SGLT2i=sodium-glucose cotransporter-2 inhibitor; T2D=type 2 diabetes", "Reference": "Marx Eur Heart J 2023", "AMA Style Guide References": "Marx Eur Heart J. 2023.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 44, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001218", "Match Text": "JARDIANCE reduced the risk of CV death independent of glucose-lowering effects and baseline HbA1c, weight, or eGFR", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "CV=cardiovascular; eGFR=estimated glomerular filtration rate; HbA1c=haemoglobin A1c.", "Reference": "2015 - Empagliflozin, Cardiovascular Outcomes, and Mortality in Type 2 Diabetes", "AMA Style Guide References": "Empagliflozin, Cardiovascular Outcomes, and Mortality in Type 2 Diabetes. 2015.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 45, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001221", "Match Text": "By the time hypertension or T2D is diagnosed, structural kidney damage may already be underway", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "eGFR=estimated  glomerular filtration rate; T2D=type 2 diabetes", "Reference": "2020 - Cardiorenal Syndrome in Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus - Rational Use f Sodium-glucose Cotransporte\n\nMárquez Nefrologia (Engl Ed) 2022\n\n2024 KDIGO Guidelines\n\n\n", "AMA Style Guide References": "Cardiorenal Syndrome in Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus - Rational Use f Sodium-glucose Cotransporte. 2020.\n\nMárquez Nefrologia (Engl Ed). 2022.\n\n2024 KDIGO Guidelines.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 46, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001229", "Match Text": "In patients with CKD, JARDIANCE slowed the eGFR decline across the spectrum of uACR", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": "Chronic kidney disease", "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "In the EMPA-KIDNEY trial, a randomised, parallel-group, double-blind, placebo-controlled study of 6609 patients with CKD, the efficacy and safety of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=3304) were evaluated vs placebo (n=3305). Patients were adults with an eGFR ≥ 20, &lt; 45 mL/min/1.73 m2 ; or an eGFR ≥ 45, &lt; 90 mL/min/1.73 m2 with a uACR ≥ 200 mg/g. The primary endpoint in the EMPA-KIDNEY trial was a composite of CV death or progression of kidney disease defined as end-stage kidney disease (the initiation of maintenance dialysis or receipt of a kidney transplant), a sustained decrease in the eGFR to &lt;10 ml/min/1.73 m2, a sustained decrease in eGFR of ≥40% from baseline, or death from renal causes. Patients treated with JARDIANCE experienced a 28% RRR in this endpoint (HR=0.72; 95% CI: 0.64, 0.82; p&lt;0.001).  Annual rate of change in eGFR was a prespecified exploratory outcome in the EMPA-KIDNEY trial. Shown are the results of long-term slope, representing the mean (±SE) changes from 2 months after the first dose of JARDIANCE or placebo to the final follow-up visit.   CI=confidence interval; CKD=chronic kidney disease; CV=cardiovascular; eGFR=estimated glomerular filtration; HR=hazard ratio; RRR=relative risk reduction; SE=standard error; uACR=urinary albumin-to-creatinine ratio.", "Reference": "Herrington 2022 NEJM, Nov 4, 2022", "AMA Style Guide References": "Herrington 2022 N Engl J Med, Nov 4, 2022.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 47, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001233", "Match Text": "2024 KDIGO guidelines reinforce SGLT2is as an early and foundational therapy for the management of CKD", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Guidelines", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "CKD=chronic kidney disease; KDIGO=Kidney Disease: Improving Global Outcomes; SGLT2i=sodium-glucose cotransporter-2 inhibitor", "Reference": "2024 KDIGO Guidelines", "AMA Style Guide References": "2024 KDIGO Guidelines.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 48, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001235", "Match Text": "Early intervention is key to help protect patients with T2D and eCVD by reducing the risk of cardio-renal complications", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Guidelines", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "eCVD=established cardiovascular disease; T2D=type 2 diabetes", "Reference": "2022 - Management of Hyperglycemia in Type 2 Diabetes, 2022. A Consensus Report by the American Diab\n\nRD-151098 JARDIANCE McDonagh, Metra 2021 ESC HF Guidelines\n\n2022 - 2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guideline for the Management of Heart Failure. JACC\n\n2020 - Global, regional, and national burden of chronic kidney disease, 1990–2017: a systematic anal", "AMA Style Guide References": "Management of Hyperglycemia in Type 2 Diabetes, 2022. A Consensus Report by the American Diab. 2022.\n\nMcDonagh, Metra 2021 ESC HF Guidelines.\n\n2022 AHA/ACC/HFSA Guideline for the Management of Heart Failure. J Am Coll Cardiol. 2022.\n\nGlobal, regional, and national burden of chronic kidney disease, 1990–2017: a systematic anal. 2020.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 49, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001236", "Match Text": "Complications can begin to develop even before diagnosis of T2D", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": "Chronic kidney disease", "Key Message Type": "CRM/Cardio-Renal-Metabolic, Disease Awareness", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": null, "Reference": "RD-118297 Diabetes Ramlo-Halsted BA, Edelman SV 5. Ramlo-Halsted BA, Edelman SV. Prim Care. 1999;26\n\nRD-143020 JARDIANCE Perspectives in Diabetes U.K. Prospective Diabetes Study 16 Overview of 6 Years'", "AMA Style Guide References": "Diabetes Ramlo-Halsted BA, Edelman SV 5. Ramlo-Halsted BA, Edelman SV. Prim Care. 1999;26.\n\nPerspectives in Diabetes Prospective UK Diabetes Study 16 Overview of 6 Years'.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 50, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001237", "Match Text": "Prescribe JARDIANCE as early, foundational T2D treatment", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": "Type 2 diabetes mellitus", "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": "HCP (Health Care Personnel)", "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "T2D=type 2 diabetes", "Reference": "Jardiance - EU/SmPC", "AMA Style Guide References": "- EU/SmPC.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 51, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001238", "Match Text": "In patients with T2D and eCVD, JARDIANCE reduced the risk of CV death independent of multiple baseline characteristics", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": "Type 2 diabetes mellitus", "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": "HCP (Health Care Personnel)", "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "In the EMPA-REG OUTCOME trial, a randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled study of 7720 patients with T2D at high cardiovascular risk, the efficacy and safety of JARDIANCE® 10 mg or 25 mg (n=4867) were evaluated vs placebo (n=2333). Patients were adults with insufficiently controlled T2D and CAD, PAD, or a history of MI or stroke. The primary composite outcome in the EMPA-REG OUTCOME trial was 3-point MACE, composed of death from CV causes, nonfatal MI, or nonfatal stroke, as analysed in the pooled JARDIANCE group vs the placebo group. The 14% RRR (1.6% ARR) in 3-point MACE (HR=0.86; 95% CI: 0.74, 0.99; p&lt;0.001 for noninferiority; p=0.04 for superiority) was driven by a reduction in the risk of CV death (HR=0.62; 95% CI: 0.49, 0.77); there was no change in risk of nonfatal MI (HR=0.87; 95% CI: 0.70, 1.09) or nonfatal stroke (HR=1.24; 95% CI: 0.92, 1.67).  A post hoc analysis of data from the EMPA-REG OUTCOME trial by baseline HbA1c subgroups. EMPA-REG OUTCOME was not powered to show differences between subgroups.  Hazard ratios are related to CV death: HbA1c &lt;7% (HR=0.30; 95% CI: 0.12, 0.80); HbA1c 7% to &lt;8% (HR=0.59; 95% CI: 0.42, 0.83); HbA1c 8% to &lt;9% (HR=0.67; 95% CI: 0.45, 0.99); HbA1c ≥9% (HR=0.76; 95% CI: 0.44, 1.31); p=0.41 for interaction. BMI &lt;30 kg/m2 (HR=0.50; 95% CI: 0.37, 0.68); BMI ≥30 kg/m2 (HR=0.78; 95% CI: 0.56, 1.08); p=0.05 for interaction.5 SBP ≥140 mm Hg and/or DBP ≥90 mm Hg (HR=0.56; 95% CI: 0.40, 0.79); SBP &lt;140 mm Hg and DBP &lt;90 mm Hg (HR=0.67; 95% CI: 0.50, 0.91); p=0.44 for interaction. eGFR ≥90 mL/min/1.73 m2 (HR=0.70; 95% CI: 0.39, 1.25); eGFR 60 to &lt;90 mL/min/1.73 m2 (HR=0.49; 95% CI: 0.35, 0.68); eGFR &lt;60 mL/min/1.73 m2 (HR=0.78; 95% CI: 0.54, 1.12); p=0.15 for interaction.  ARR=absolute risk reduction; BMI=body mass index; BP=blood pressure; CAD=coronary artery disease; CI=confidence interval; CV=cardiovascular; DBP=diastolic blood pressure; eCVD=established cardiovascular disease; eGFR=estimated glomerular filtration rate; HbA1c=haemoglobin A1c; HR=hazard ratio; MACE=major adverse cardiovascular events; MI=myocardial infarction; PAD=peripheral artery disease; SBP=systolic blood pressure; T2D=type 2 diabetes.", "Reference": "2015 - Empagliflozin, Cardiovascular Outcomes, and Mortality in Type 2 Diabetes\n\nRD-154658 JARDIANCE INZUCCHI CIRCULATION\n", "AMA Style Guide References": "Empagliflozin, Cardiovascular Outcomes, and Mortality in Type 2 Diabetes. 2015.\n\nINZUCCHI CIRCULATION.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 52, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001240", "Match Text": "In insulin-naive patients with T2D and eCVD, JARDIANCE reduced the need for insulin initiation", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": "Type 2 diabetes mellitus", "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": "HCP (Health Care Personnel)", "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "Adult patients with insufficiently controlled T2D and CAD, PAD, or a history of MI or stroke  Outcome of insulin initiation was defined to be maintained on ≥ 2 consecutive visits ≥ 13 weeks apart. Cox regression model adjusted for baseline HbA1c, time since T2D diagnosis, BMI, eGFR, geographic region, and treatment status.Kaplan-Meier estimates  BMI=body mass index; CAD=coronary artery disease; CI=confidence interval; CV=cardiovascular; eCVD=established cardiovascular disease; eGFR=estimated glomerular filtration rate; HR=hazard ratio; MI=myocardial infarction; PAD=peripheral artery disease; RRR=relative risk reduction; T2D=type 2 diabetes", "Reference": "2015 - Empagliflozin, Cardiovascular Outcomes, and Mortality in Type 2 Diabetes\n\nRD-152033 JARDIANCE Vaduganathan Vaduganathan DOM 2021 ", "AMA Style Guide References": "Empagliflozin, Cardiovascular Outcomes, and Mortality in Type 2 Diabetes. 2015.\n\nVaduganathan Vaduganathan Diabetes Obes Metab. 2021.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 53, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001241", "Match Text": "In insulin-treated patients with T2D and eCVD, JARDIANCE reduced the need for large increases in insulin dose", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": "Type 2 diabetes mellitus", "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": "HCP (Health Care Personnel)", "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "Adult patients with insufficiently controlled T2D and CAD, PAD, or a history of MI or stroke.  Sustained insulin dose increase was defined as time to first increase in total daily insulin dose by &gt;20% from baseline total daily insulin dose, sustained for at least 2 consecutive visits at a minimum of 13 weeks apart, analysed in patients treated with insulin at baseline  CAD=coronary artery disease; CI=confidence interval; CV=cardiovascular; eCVD=established cardiovascular disease; HR=hazard ratio; MI=myocardial infarction; PAD=peripheral artery disease; RRR=relative risk reduction; T2D=type 2 diabetes", "Reference": "2015 - Empagliflozin, Cardiovascular Outcomes, and Mortality in Type 2 Diabetes\n\nRD-152033 JARDIANCE Vaduganathan Vaduganathan DOM 2021 ", "AMA Style Guide References": "Empagliflozin, Cardiovascular Outcomes, and Mortality in Type 2 Diabetes. 2015.\n\nVaduganathan Vaduganathan Diabetes Obes Metab. 2021.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 54, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001242", "Match Text": "JARDIANCE provides multiple metabolic benefits to patients with T2D while also reducing CV and renal risk", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": "Type 2 diabetes mellitus", "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": "HCP (Health Care Personnel)", "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "In a 24-week, double-blind, placebo-controlled study of 637 patients with T2D, the efficacy and safety of JARDIANCE 10 mg (n=217) and JARDIANCE 25 mg (n=213) as add-on therapy to metformin ≥1500 mg were evaluated vs placebo added to metformin (n=207). The primary endpoint was adjusted mean change (SE) from baseline in HbA1c (%); weight loss and blood pressure reduction were key secondary and exploratory endpoints, respectively.  CV=cardiovascular; SE=standard error of the mean; T2D=type 2 diabetes.", "Reference": "5. Häring HU, Merker L, Seewaldt-Becker E, et al Diabetes Care. 2014;36:1650-1659. RD-105019\n\nRD-145767 Inzucchi SE, Davies MJ, Khunti K, et al. Empagliflozin treatment 2021;33:425-433.\n\nJardiance - EU/SmPC", "AMA Style Guide References": "Häring HU, Merker L, Seewaldt-Becker E, et al. Diabetes Care. 2014;36:1650-1659.\n\nInzucchi SE, Davies MJ, Khunti K, et al. Empagliflozin treatment 2021;33:425-433.\n\n- EU/SmPC.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 55, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001243", "Match Text": "JARDIANCE can be used down to an eGFR of 30 mL/min/1.73 m2 in patients with T2D", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": "Type 2 diabetes mellitus", "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": "HCP (Health Care Personnel)", "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "Please see the SmPC for the full list of AEs and full list of special warnings and precautions for use.  AE=adverse event; eGFR=estimated glomerular ﬁltration rate; SmPC=summary of product characteristics; T2D=type 2 diabetes", "Reference": "Jardiance - EU/SmPC", "AMA Style Guide References": "- EU/SmPC.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 56, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001244", "Match Text": "Diabetes guidelines recommend early use of SGLT2is in patients with T2D regardless of metabolic parameters", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": "Type 2 diabetes mellitus", "Key Message Type": "Guidelines", "End Audience": "HCP (Health Care Personnel)", "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "In addition to comprehensive CV risk management  ASCVD has been defined differently across CVOTs but all included individuals with eCVD (eg, MI, stroke, any revascularisation procedure). Variably included: conditions such as transient ischaemic attack, unstable angina, amputation, symptomatic or asymptomatic coronary artery disease. A strong recommendation is warranted for people with CVD and a weaker recommendation for those with indicators of high CV risk. While definitions of high risk vary, most comprise ≥55 years of age with 2 or more additional risk factors (including obesity, hypertension, smoking, dyslipidaemia, or albuminuria). Current or prior symptoms of HF with documented HFrEF or HFpEF. eGFR &lt;60 mL/min/1.73 m2 OR albuminuria (ACR ≥3.0 mg/mmol [30 mg/g]). These measurements may vary over time; thus, a repeat measure is required to document CKD.   ACR=albumin to creatinine ratio; ASCVD=atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease; ADA=American Diabetes Association; CV=cardiovascular; CVD=cardiovascular disease; CVOT=cardiovascular outcomes trial; CKD=chronic kidney disease; EASD=European Association for the Study of Diabetes; eGFR=estimated glomerular filtration rate; HF=heart failure; HFpEF=heart failure with preserved ejection fraction; HFrEF=heart failure with reduced ejection fraction; SGLT2i=sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor; T2D=type 2 diabetes", "Reference": "2022 - Management of Hyperglycemia in Type 2 Diabetes, 2022. A Consensus Report by the American Diab", "AMA Style Guide References": "Management of Hyperglycemia in Type 2 Diabetes, 2022. A Consensus Report by the American Diab. 2022.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 57, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001245", "Match Text": "JARDIANCE demonstrated its proven safety profile across a broad population of patients with CKD.", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Safety", "End Audience": "HCP (Health Care Personnel)", "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "Adult patients with an eGFR ≥20, &lt;45 mL/min/1.73 m2; or an eGFR ≥45, &lt;90 mL/min/1.73 m2 with a uACR ≥200 mg/g  CKD=chronic kidney disease; eGFR=estimated glomerular filtration rate; uACR=urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio.", "Reference": "Jardiance - EU/SmPC\n\nHerrington 2022 NEJM, Nov 4, 2022", "AMA Style Guide References": "- EU/SmPC.\n\nHerrington 2022 N Engl J Med, Nov 4, 2022.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 58, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001246", "Match Text": "The safety profile seen in EMPA-KIDNEY was consistent with the overall safety profile for JARDIANCE", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Safety", "End Audience": "HCP (Health Care Personnel)", "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "Please see the Summary of Product Characteristics for complete safety information.", "Reference": "Jardiance - EU/SmPC", "AMA Style Guide References": "- EU/SmPC.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 59, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001288", "Match Text": "JARDIANCE can help you stay committed by: offering protection by reducing the risk of CV death for adult patients with T2D + eCVD; reducing the risk of CV death and HHF for adult patients with HF; reducing the risk of kidney disease progression or CV death in adult patients with CKD; and lowering HbA1c in adult patients with T2D.", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Efficacy", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "CKD=chronic kidney disease; CV=cardiovascular; eCVD=established cardiovascular disease; HbA1c=haemoglobin A1c; HF=heart failure; HHF=hospitalisation for heart failure; T2D=type 2 diabetes.", "Reference": "Jardiance - EU/SmPC", "AMA Style Guide References": "- EU/SmPC.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 60, "cells": {"Resource": "Sunil", "Name": "CL-CRP-001289", "Match Text": "Your patients with CKD are more likely to die from CVD than to progress to kidney failure", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "CRM/Cardio-Renal-Metabolic, Disease Awareness", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "CKD=chronic kidney disease; CVD=cardiovascular disease.", "Reference": "Vondenhoff Herz 2024", "AMA Style Guide References": "Vondenhoff Herz. 2024.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 61, "cells": {"Resource": "Moumita", "Name": "CL-CRP-001290", "Match Text": "Nearly 1 in 2 patients with CKD will die from CVD before reaching end-stage kidney disease and requiring dialysis", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "CRM/Cardio-Renal-Metabolic, Disease Awareness", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "CKD=chronic kidney disease; CV=cardiovascular; CVD=cardiovascular disease", "Reference": "Vondenhoff Herz 2024\n\n2021 - Cardiovascular Disease in Chronic    _______Kidney Disease\n\nYu Kidney Med 2023", "AMA Style Guide References": "Vondenhoff Herz. 2024.\n\nCardiovascular Disease in Chronic Kidney Disease. 2021.\n\nYu Kidney Med. 2023.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 62, "cells": {"Resource": "Moumita", "Name": "CL-CRP-001291", "Match Text": "CV mortality risk is elevated even at low albuminuria levels", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "CRM/Cardio-Renal-Metabolic, Disease Awareness", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "CV=cardiovascular", "Reference": "Matsushita Nat Rev Nephrol 2022", "AMA Style Guide References": "Matsushita Nat Rev Nephrol. 2022.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 63, "cells": {"Resource": "Moumita", "Name": "CL-CRP-001293", "Match Text": "JARDIANCE is an oral treatment that can seamlessly transition into your patient’s daily regimen", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "Dosing and Administration", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": null, "Reference": "Jardiance - EU/SmPC", "AMA Style Guide References": "- EU/SmPC.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 64, "cells": {"Resource": "Moumita", "Name": "CL-CRP-001374", "Match Text": "Your patients with T2D may be at increased risk of CV death", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "CRM/Cardio-Renal-Metabolic", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "CV=cardiovascular; T2D=type 2 diabetes", "Reference": "Raghavan J Am Heart Assoc 2019\n\nRD-118297 Diabetes Ramlo-Halsted BA, Edelman SV 5. Ramlo-Halsted BA, Edelman SV. Prim Care. 1999;26(\n\nRD-143020 JARDIANCE Perspectives in DiabetesU.K. Prospective Diabetes Study 16Overview of 6 Years'", "AMA Style Guide References": "Raghavan J Am Heart Assoc. 2019.\n\nDiabetes Ramlo-Halsted BA, Edelman SV 5. Ramlo-Halsted BA, Edelman SV. Prim Care. 1999;26(.\n\nPerspectives in DiabetesU.Prospective K Diabetes Study 16Overview of 6 Years'.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 65, "cells": {"Resource": "Moumita", "Name": "CL-CRP-001381", "Match Text": "JARDIANCE can be initiated down to an eGFR of 20 mL/min/1.73 m2 in patients with T2D + eCVD, CKD, or HF.", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": "Type 2 diabetes mellitus", "Key Message Type": "Dosing and Administration", "End Audience": "HCP (Health Care Personnel)", "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "Please see the SmPC for the full list of AEs and full list of special warnings and precautions for use.  AE=adverse event; CKD=chronic kidney disease; eCVD=established cardiovascular disease; eGFR=estimated glomerular ﬁltration rate; HF=heart failure; SmPC=summary of product characteristics; T2D=type 2 diabetes", "Reference": "Jardiance - EU/SmPC", "AMA Style Guide References": "- EU/SmPC.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 66, "cells": {"Resource": "Moumita", "Name": "CL-CRP-001375", "Match Text": "Irreversible vascular damage and complications can begin to develop years before T2D is diagnosed", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "CRM/Cardio-Renal-Metabolic, Disease Awareness", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "T2D=type 2 diabetes", "Reference": "Palladino BMJ Open Diabetes Res Care 2020\n\nAlexander Cardiovasc Res 2021\n\nSun Front Pharmacol 2020\n\nHahad J Am Heart Assoc 2019", "AMA Style Guide References": "Palladino BMJ Open Diabetes Res Care. 2020.\n\nAlexander Cardiovasc Res. 2021.\n\nSun Front Pharmacol. 2020.\n\nHahad J Am Heart Assoc. 2019.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 67, "cells": {"Resource": "Moumita", "Name": "CL-CRP-001377", "Match Text": "Patients with an HbA1c of 7%-7.9% have a 2x higher risk of CV death", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "CRM/Cardio-Renal-Metabolic, Disease Awareness", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "Baseline reference: HbA1c 6%-6.9%.  CV=cardiovascular; HbA1c=haemoglobin A1c; T2D=type 2 diabetes", "Reference": "Raghavan J Am Heart Assoc 2019", "AMA Style Guide References": "Raghavan J Am Heart Assoc. 2019.", "Content Modules &amp; Rule Sets": "NA"}}, {"row_number": 68, "cells": {"Resource": "Moumita", "Name": "CL-CRP-001376", "Match Text": "1 out of 2 patients with T2D die from CV disease", "Country of Origin": "Corporate", "Brand": "JARDIANCE", "Therapeutic Area": "Metabolism", "Lifecycle State": "Approved for Use", "Indication": null, "Key Message Type": "CRM/Cardio-Renal-Metabolic, Disease Awareness", "End Audience": null, "Contextual Information": null, "Footnotes": "CV=cardio